Cost
----
In the interest of improving awareness of hurricanes and tropical
storms, there is NO cost for either personal or commercial use
of TrackStm.

Request for Volunteers
----------------------
Hoping to tap into the spirit of group cooperation and voluntarism,
I am requesting any voluntary assistance that may be offered
for the improvement of TrackStm for all of us.

If you are so motivated, please e-mail your specific interest in
enhancing TrackStm to myself, Mark Ulrich, 75226,2447.
See the list which follows for a collection of ideas.

For those volunteers that contact me expressing a desire to contribute,
I will attempt to: E-mail the contributor a copy of the TrackStm
OPL source code (if needed), assure that redundant contributions
are not being performed, maintain the "master" source code, and
periodically re-issue TrackStm to CompuServe's Palmtop forum.

Specific enhancement ideas for TrackStm include - but are by no means
limited to - the following:

1.  Use OPL's sprite graphics to add a "spinning" hurricane rather than
    the current filled square at each new data point.
2.  Provide an optional "automated" mode that would use a user-input
    delay to automatically advance the hurricane from the first through
    the final data point.
3.  Add optional "sound effects" to the program.  Creativity welcomed!
4.  Incorporate a help file that describes the five-categories of
    hurricanes, along with the storm's destructive potential.
5.  Pre-load a selection of past hurricanes to permit the user to
    view previous storms.
6.  Make use of the Psion's shades of grey to permit multiple storm
    tracks to be simultaneously displayed on-screen.
7.  Presuming item 5, add a "database" function to TrackStm whereby
    the user can select - for example, "all storms which hit within
    100 miles of Galveston, and were category 4 or better on impact,
    landed during the month of October, and whose track took them
    within 500 miles of St. Thomas."
8.  Modify the GULF.PIC bit-map to depict major populated centers 
    along the Gulf of Mexico.
9.  Add an introductory screen.
10. Convert the OPO file an APP file.
11. Develop a setup executible to automate the installation of TrackStm,
    thereby eliminating the need for the user to create subdirectories,
    copy to specific locations, etc.  Needless to say, these steps tend
    to be a significant demotivator - especially for new users.
12. Expand the size of the GULF.PIC to permit a "scrolling" effect allowing
    the storm to be viewed as it moves (for example) across the Atlantic,
    through the Caribean, and into the Gulf.


